-- title:
--   The machine program fwP computes the Fermat-ring convolution fermatW
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be HvdH parameters with $P\ge 1$, $K \le f_n$, $\mathrm{logOuter} \le f_n$, $\mathrm{logOuter}\le kb$, and let $M$ be a multiplication spec that multiplies $(f_n+1)$-bit integers at cost $\le Φ_m$. Let $U, V$ be lists of $2^{\mathrm{logOuter}}\cdot r$ Gaussian integers ($r = 2^e$) with all coordinates of absolute value $<2^P$, stored as item lists on stacks `GU` and `VI`, and let the constant registers hold their prescribed values ($2^P$, $r$, $2^{2rP}$, $2^{f_n}+1$, $2^{f_n}$, $2^{\mathrm{logOuter}}$, $kb$, $K-1$, $2^{f_n-\mathrm{logOuter}}$, and the encoded axis descriptors of the outer axes). Then the program `fwP` runs from $s$ (calling the multiplication procedure for the pointwise products), consumes $U$ and $V$, and pushes `fermatW q U V` — the cyclic convolution of the published reference computed by Kronecker packing into the Fermat ring $\mathbb Z/(2^{f_n}+1)$, forward DIF transforms, pointwise products, DIT transform and unpacking — onto `OUT`, with cost at most `fwCost q kb Φm`.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.FW.fwP_spec` of the Lean development NLogN/HFW10.lean (lines 137–355), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

